The slop patterns in ats/slop_patterns.py are adapted from the no-ai-slop skill.

    Source:  https://github.com/petergyang/no-ai-slop
    Author:  Peter Yang
    Licence: MIT (see LICENSE in this directory)

SKILL.md is vendored verbatim so the adaptation is auditable and upstream changes
can be diffed in.

Two deliberate departures, both documented in ats/slop_patterns.py:

1. Scope. The source targets prose. A resume bullet is a different form, so each
   pattern declares where it applies. Prose-only patterns (kickers, recap endings,
   throat-clearing) run against the summary section, never against bullets.

2. Trimmed lexicon. Terms that predate LLMs and merely read as formal -- notably
   "spearheaded" -- are excluded, so the detector flags machine phrasing rather
   than formal writing.

The source's governing rule is adopted as-is and shapes the whole product: "Do not
rewrite, score the draft, or guess whether AI wrote it. AI detectors guess. Named
patterns are evidence the user can check." Nothing in this tool emits an
AI-likelihood score.
